6. AUTOMATIC READINESS CHECK
==================================================

Once both sector and scenario are selected:
- Automatically evaluate readiness for the exact combination of:
  calculation identifier + sector identifier + scenario identifier.
- Use existing backend readiness rules.
- Display:
  “Checking required configurations…”
- Prevent duplicate requests for an unchanged selection.
- Ignore or cancel stale responses when selections change.

Do not require a separate initial Check Readiness click.

If readiness passes:
- Show:
  “The selected sector and scenario meet the required configuration checks.
   You can proceed with the calculation.”
- Display an enabled Calculate button.

If readiness fails:
- Show “Not Ready”.
- Keep Calculate hidden.
- Expand a Readiness Issues panel below the context controls.
- Show actual failed checks, missing configurations or inputs, and
  backend-provided corrective guidance.
- Provide “Retry Readiness”.

If the readiness request fails:
- Show:
  “Unable to verify readiness. Please retry.”
- Keep Calculate hidden.
- Do not report prerequisite success or failure without a valid result.

Changing calculation, sector, or scenario:
- Invalidates previous readiness.
- Clears previous readiness issues.
- Hides Calculate until the new selection passes.
- Clears unrelated execution details.

Never show successful readiness alongside empty dropdown placeholders.

==================================================
7. CALCULATE ACTION
==================================================

When Calculate is clicked:
- Submit execution for the exact context that passed readiness.
- Prevent duplicate submissions.
- Reveal the execution container ABOVE Calculation Context.
- Display “Starting execution” while submission is pending.
- Keep stages pending until backend status confirms activity.

Once the backend returns the execution:
- Display its actual run ID and status.
- Associate it with its calculation, sector, and scenario identifiers.
- Start live status/event monitoring.

If submission fails:
- Show the actual error.
- Do not fabricate a run ID or running state.
- Restore the appropriate action state.

If the backend rejects the start because prerequisites changed:
- Invalidate readiness.
- Show returned readiness issues.
- Require readiness to pass again.

Bring the execution container into view when execution starts.
Do not repeatedly reposition the page as live updates arrive.

==================================================
8. EXECUTION CONTAINER POSITION
==================================================

During execution, use this main content order:

1. Existing title, description, and clock/history icon.
2. Calculation Execution container.
   - Run metadata.
   - Four-stage stepper.
   - Live events beneath the stepper.
3. Calculation Context container.
   - Actual sector and scenario.
   - Readiness information.
   - Disabled “Calculation Running” action.

The execution container must be ABOVE the sector/scenario controls.

Before execution:
- Show context controls without the execution container.

After execution starts:
- Open the execution container at the top of the workflow.

==================================================
9. EXECUTION METADATA AND STEPPER
==================================================

Execution container heading:
“Calculation Execution”

Display:
- Run ID.
- Overall status.
- Sector.
- Scenario.
- Start time, if available.
- Elapsed duration, if available.
- Last successful update time.

Use four numbered stages:

1. Validation
2. Calculation
3. Reconciliation
4. Completion

Readiness is a pre-execution requirement, not a numbered execution stage.

Stage states:
- Pending.
- Running.
- Completed.
- Failed.

Appearance:
- Running: existing blue status styling, active indicator, spinner,
  “Running” label.
- Completed: existing success styling, checkmark, “Completed” label.
- Pending: existing neutral styling, stage number, “Pending” label.
- Failed: existing failure styling, error icon, “Failed” label.

The currently running stage must be clearly highlighted in blue using the
existing application theme.

Use actual backend state.
Do not advance stages using artificial timers.

==================================================
10. LIVE EVENTS UNDER THE STEPPER
==================================================

Inside the execution container, directly below the stepper, display the
active stage’s live events.

Headings:
- “Validation — Live Events”
- “Calculation — Live Events”
- “Reconciliation — Live Events”
- “Completion — Live Events”

When Validation starts:
- Highlight Stage 1 blue.
- Show its actual validation events.

When Calculation starts:
- Mark Validation completed.
- Highlight Stage 2 blue.
- Show Calculation events.

Apply the same behavior for Reconciliation and Completion.

Event fields:
Time | Activity | Status | Message

Use actual backend events.
Do not invent validations, task names, percentages, or results.

Support:
- Incremental updates.
- Deduplication using event IDs or available sequence information.
- Chronological ordering.
- Loading states.
- “Waiting for stage events…” when no events are available.
- Preservation of received events for the displayed run.

Allow users to select a stage to inspect its events.

When users inspect an earlier stage:
- Keep the active-stage indicator visible.
- Provide “View Active Stage”.
- Do not forcibly replace the inspected stage on every update.

If auto-scrolling is implemented:
- Do not interrupt users reading older events.
- Provide “Follow live events” to resume following updates.

Use existing supported transport:
- SSE, if provided.
- WebSockets, if provided.
- Configurable polling otherwise.

Inspect existing backend capabilities before choosing transport.

