11. NAVIGATION DURING EXECUTION
==================================================

Users can switch to other calculation menu items while an execution
continues.

Example:
- Start Tax ETR.
- Navigate to OCI Tax.
- Use the OCI Tax screen.
- Return to Tax ETR.
- See the same Tax ETR run’s current live status.

Execution lifecycle must not depend on whether its dashboard component
is currently visible.

When navigating away:
- Clean up view-specific subscriptions where appropriate.
- Do not cancel the backend run.
- Do not mark the run complete or failed.
- Retain the information needed to retrieve the run again.

Store active-run references in an existing shared service/store rather than
only in component-local state.

Backend state is the source of truth.
Client storage may retain run IDs but must not be treated as authoritative
execution status.

Do not create duplicate live subscriptions when navigating repeatedly.

==================================================
12. RESTORE THE LAST ACTIVE EXECUTION
==================================================

When returning to a calculation:
- Retrieve its latest relevant active run using existing backend
  capabilities and access rules.
- Reconcile retained client references with backend state.
- Display “Restoring execution status…” while loading.
- Restore the execution container above context.
- Populate its actual sector and scenario.
- Show its current stage and available events.
- Resume live updates.

Do not start a new calculation when restoring a run.
Do not reset a running job to Stage 1.

If it completed while the user was elsewhere:
- Show Completed.
- Show the completion message.
- Enable Close Execution.

If it failed:
- Show the failed stage and error details.

If multiple active runs are supported:
- Follow backend concurrency rules.
- Restore the latest relevant active run by default.
- Allow other accessible runs to be inspected through history.
- Never mix metadata or events from different runs.

If the required active-run lookup is unavailable:
- Identify the missing integration explicitly.
- Do not simulate persistence as if it were implemented.

==================================================
13. CONTEXT DURING AN ACTIVE RUN
==================================================

Show the displayed run’s actual sector and scenario.

Disable editing of that run’s context while it is active.
Display a disabled “Calculation Running” action.

Do not disable the vertical calculation menu.

Ensure dropdown values and option identifiers use matching types.
Restore selected values after asynchronous option loading.

Do not show “Select sector” or “Select scenario” for a populated active run.
If necessary, temporarily show read-only context until dropdown options load.

Prevent duplicate execution according to backend concurrency rules.

==================================================
14. COMPLETION AND CLOSING EXECUTION
==================================================

After all required stages complete successfully:
- Mark all stages completed.
- Show:
  “Calculation completed successfully.”
- Stop live monitoring for that terminal run.
- Keep events available for review.
- Enable Close Execution and/or a close icon on the execution container.

Closing a completed execution panel:
- Hides the execution container.
- Returns to the title, description, and context-selection view.
- Does not delete the run.
- Does not remove history.
- Does not cancel anything.

Keep the run accessible through the history clock icon.

Do not reopen a completed panel simply because the component rerenders
after the user closed it.

On later navigation back:
- Automatically restore an active execution, if one exists.
- Otherwise show the context-selection view.
- Keep completed runs accessible through history.

Failed terminal runs may also be closed after inspection.

Do not add cancellation for active executions unless supported by the
backend and separately required.

